From 6ad10227e2b36d6b1e46f5bd9ecf8c4bb57b7466 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: TheKit <thekit@disroot.org>
Date: Sun, 16 Feb 2025 15:56:18 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 1/9] hybris: introduce thunk-based TLS access patcher for
 aarch64
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Some Android graphics drivers (noticed with libGLES_mali.so) make use of private
bionic TLS slots. Since these slots use fixed offsets from the thread ID
register, the access routine is inlined by the compiler and cannot be hooked by
libhybris symbol overriding.

The code compiled with glibc has no notion of bionic's reserved TLS area, so
these slots may be used by the executable/other libraries. While
libtls-padding.so worked when TLS was relocatable, it fails when the main
executable uses local-exec TLS model, where access is inlined similarly to
bionic and can't be relocated by the linker.

This causes the application and GLES driver to overwrite each other's TLS data,
leading to crashes.

Introduce thunk-based TLS access patching support into libhybris to work around
this. When HYBRIS_TLS_PATCH=1, all libhybris linker-loaded code is analyzed for
MRS TPIDR_EL0 instructions. When found, the instruction is replaced with a
branch to a dynamically generated thunk that:

1. Reads the thread pointer (mrs x<rt>, tpidr_el0)
2. Adds the libhybris TLS offset (add x<rt>, x<rt>, #offset)
3. Branches back to the next instruction

The linker allocates thunk regions immediately after loading the library,
which should stay within the ±128MB range limit for the branch instruction
as long as the library is smaller than that.

HYBRIS_TLS_PATCH can also be set to a colon-separated list of library filenames
to limit the performance impact and avoid breaking unrelated code, e.g.:

HYBRIS_TLS_PATCH=libGLES_mali.so
